```js
export default function solve(params) {
  const t0 = Date.now();
  const TIME_LIMIT = 540000;
  const N = 26;
  let trace = [];
  let lastTraceT = 0;

  function nowSec(){ return (Date.now()-t0)/1000; }
  function timeUp(){ return Date.now() - t0 > TIME_LIMIT; }

  // seeded rng for reproducibility? Math.random fine. Use mulberry32 for reproducible behavior; different each run fine either way. I'll use Math.random.

  function clone(a){ let b = new Array(a.length); for (let i=0;i<a.length;i++){ b[i] = [a[i][0], a[i][1], a[i][2]]; } return b; }

  function extract(obj, depth){
    if (!obj || depth > 4) return null;
    if (Array.isArray(obj)){
      if (obj.length === N && Array.isArray(obj[0]) && typeof obj[0][0] === 'number' && obj[0].length >= 3) return obj;
      for (let i=0;i<Math.min(obj.length, 64); i++){ let r = extract(obj[i], depth+1); if (r) return r; }
      return null;
    }
    if (typeof obj === 'object'){
      for (let k in obj){ let r = extract(obj[k], depth+1); if (r) return r; }
    }
    return null;
  }
```

Careful: extract on array whose elements are arrays — the check `obj.length===N && Array.isArray(obj[0])...` catches [ [x,y,r], ...]. If params = {solution:[[...]], trace:[...]}, recursion through values finds it. Also numbers array length 3. Good. But avoid infinite recursion on huge params (depth guard). Also `for k in obj` on arrays handled above. OK.

