Let me also double-check one more possible REAL bug in the new bandit code:

```
          var scp = moveScore(cand, ta);
```

moveScore MUTATES cand and returns scoreOf — so after up-to-4 probes, cand has been mutated several times and `s` may have been replaced by the LAST improving probe's score — fine, cand consistent with final s ✓ (s updates only when scp > s, and cand at that moment holds that config; subsequent non-improving probes mutate cand but s stays — MISMATCH: cand (mutated) vs s (older probe's score)! Then `if (s > bestS + 5e-13) { best = clone(cand) }` would store cand ≠ the config that scored s!)

**FOUND IT — THE REAL BUG.** In the bandit chain: when probe A improves (s = scp_A, cand = config_A), then probe B (non-improving) MUTATES cand away from config_A. At the end, `s` corresponds to config_A but `cand` corresponds to config_A + mutation_B (+possibly more). Then:
- `if (s > bestS)` → `best = clone(cand)` — stores the WRONG config (mutated after the score was taken) while bestS keeps s!
- `bestPool.push([s, clone(cand)])` — same mismatch.

THIS is the leak: bestS says 2.635797931 (config_A's score) but best stores config_A mutated by later probes — which repaired to 2.635756720. Magnitude 4.1e-5 — matches a perturb-level score shift. Also pool/elite polluted similarly.

Fix: clone-snapshot when a probe improves, restore at chain end:

```
        var chainBestS = s; var chainBestC = null;
        ...if (scp > s) { s = scp; chainBestC = clone(cand); piecesN = 0; }
        ...after loop: if (chainBestC) for (i...) cand = chainBestC? 
```
Simplest: after the while loop, if last improvement happened, set cand = the snapshot:
```
        if (chainBestC !== null) { cand = chainBestC; }
```
where chainBestC updated on each improving probe. But also non-improving probes mutated cand — snapshot only on improvement ✓, then final cand = last improving snapshot (or if no probe improved, cand is mutated but s unchanged from the ORIGINAL scoreOf before probes — which no longer matches mutated cand either!). So: snapshot BEFORE the chain: `var preS = s; var preC = clone(cand);` and after: if chainBestC → cand = chainBestC (s = its score ✓); else → cand = preC (s unchanged ✓). 

Actually simpler: instead of mutating `cand` in probes, run probes on a local working copy: `var work = clone(cand); scp = moveScore(work, ta); if (scp > s) { s = scp; cand = work; ... }` — cand only ever replaced by the improving config. Non-improvers discarded. Clean, one-line-ish fix.

Also `pool.push([scp, clone(cand)])` — should push `clone(work)`. My current code pushes cand (mismatch again).

Same mismatch issue ANYWHERE else? `moveScore` also used in `adaptiveKick(c)` → `perturb` tag band (u in [0.76,0.84)): `return adaptiveKick(c);` — adaptiveKick mutates c and returns a score — but perturb's return value is IGNORED at call sites (`perturb(cand, strength)`) → harmless (c ends mutated, which is the intent of perturb).

